rd if you  have customers you charge customers a  fee for connecting buyers and sellers  and it's as simple as that if FTX just  stuck to this they likely wouldn't have  run into any issues at all but as you  might have guessed brokerages often get  greedy and look from creative ways for  generating extra Revenue in the case of  FDX this can be traced back to two risky  practices the first of which being a  non-cash collateral whenever you have  cash laying around at a bank brokerage  or really any financial institution for  that matter it's rarely kept as cash  usually the institution will take the  cash and invest it to generate more  Revenue they figured that only a very  small portion of their customers will  actually be withdrawing money at any  given time so they keep some cash  collateral for this but the rest gets  invested usually most traditional  institutions are forced by law to keep  this collateral and cash but when we're  talking about crypto brokerages located  in the Bahamas the rules aren't as clear  Sam decided that it was a great idea to  keep this money not in US Dollars Euros  pounds or even Bitcoin he decided to  keep this money in a token that FTX  themselves created called FTD for the  longest time this seemed like a  brilliant play Given that the ftt token  had rallied from just a few dollars to  seventy dollars but the thing to note is  that such tokens not only outperform  Bitcoin to the upside but also to the  downside and let's just say Bitcoin  hasn't been performing so well this  itself likely added significant pressure  onto Sam's companies but the real crisis  didn't come until people started  pointing this out on the internet for  obvious reasons FTX customers started to  feel a bit uneasy with FTX storing so  much money in the ftt token and the nail  in the coffin came when bayan's founder  sees he announced that they were going  to liquidate on love their FDT within  the next couple of months this  essentially sparked a bank runner on an  FDT causing FDT to fall back to where it  started meaning that fgx's collateral  was now worth a lot less ideally FTX  should just be able to sell their  Investments and to make up for any  discrepancies in withdrawals but that  brings us into their second risky  practice which includes highly volatile  leverage Investments usually financial  institutions investor reserves into  highly liquid safe Investments like  money market funds bonds and mortgages  in the past we've seen that even that  can lead to issues so it's no wonder why  using these reserves to engage and  leverage crypto trading at led to issues  and the worst part about all of this is  that Financial stress often operates in  negative feedback loops when customers  hear that a given institution may be  illiquid they get scared and it would  rather money this leads to the  institution truly becoming illiquited  even if they weren't deliquated before  and given that most of the influencers  who pitched FDX are not telling  investors to withdraw their money well  if everything else didn't already cause  a financial crisis at FTX this  definitely did and let's just say it's  bad it's 8 billion dollars bad  if the 8 billion deficit didn't convince  you that the FTX situation was bad just  listen to what Sam had tried to do Sam  was so desperate to get a bailout that  he approached his biggest competitor  finance and asked them to buy a FTX out  at first binance agreed as this seemed  like a slam dunk Finance was already the  largest crypto brokerage by far in terms  of trading volume and acquiring FTX  would just further solidify their  position but it just took one look at  ftx's balance sheet for binance to  realize that this company was something  that they'd like to stay far far away  from binance would make an official  statement that they would not pursue the  FDX acquisition due to mishandling of  customer funds and alleged U.S agency  investigations and that brings us to  where we are today with the same  scrambling to put together 8 billion  dollars would just by no means an easy  feat even for FTX here's the thing even  at the peak FTX was only worth 12.3  billion dollars so Sam is essentially  asking investors to pay 66 of their Peak  valuation just to bail out the company  who knows how much more money they'll  need to survive this Exodus of customers  from an Investor's perspective it makes  no sense to pay so much money and  voluntarily take on such a massive  gamble in fact existing investors are  already doing the exact opposite by  writing off FTX as a complete loss  Sequoia for example sent out a letter to  their Partners stating quote based on  our current understanding we are marking  our investment down to zero dollars and  if you can to convince existing  investors who already have skin in the  game to invest more money and save their  investment and it's gonna be virtually  impossible to convince anyone else so  personally I think it's just a matter of  time until FTX does officially go  bankrupt if they haven't already by the  time you're watching this and even if by  some miracle they survive you probably  don't want to be keeping your crypto at  an institution that doesn't even believe  in crypto and now has a history of  mismanagement  whether FTX does end up collapsing or  not you can bet that Regulators are  going to use the situation as  justification to crack down on the  crypto industry and honestly that's  probably for the best there's a reason  that bangs and traditional brokerages  are regulated so heavily and it's  because if they aren't it usually leads  to end customers like you and me being  hurt we've already seen two brokerages  collapse this year Celsius and a voyager  and the FTX may just be an addition to  that list and the truth is while I'm  sure Sam is devastated that his company  is on the brink of collabs and that his  net worth has crumbled from 27 billion  dollars to not even a billion and it's  not like anything really changes for him  he still has a seven and a half percent  stake in Robin Hood and unlike FTX Robin  hooded actually has cash in fact Robin  Hood has 10 billion dollars in cash and  their cash reserves are literally worth  more than their market cap so and it's  more than likely that Robin Hood it will  survive this recession and to see the  light of day at which point Sam will not  only become a billionaire again but a  multi-billionaire and even a Deca  billionaire the ones that are truly left  holding the bags are ftx's 5 million  customers or just 3.5 million customers  and Celsius is nearly 2 million  customers I mean it these three  companies alone will have affected over  10 million people and that's just the  people affected by their direct Fallout  if we include indirect effects it's  likely tens of millions more and if  crypto really is the future it appears  to me that regulation is a must but  that's just what I think do you agree  comment that down below also drop a like  if you hope FTX customers get their  money back and of course consider  checking out our Discord Community to  suggest for everybody ideas and to  consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then I'm Hari and I'll see you guys on  the next one  [Music]
 

